Atomic NumberWhat it is: The number of protons found in the nucleus of an atom of that element.Where it is: Usually located in the top-left or top-center corner of the tile. It determines the element's position and unique identity on the table.Chemical SymbolWhat it is: A one-, two-, or three-letter abbreviation representing the element (e.g., H for Hydrogen, Na for Sodium, Au for Gold). The first letter is always capitalized, and any subsequent letters are lowercase.Where it is: Centered prominently in the middle of the tile in a larger font. Many symbols are derived from Latin or Greek names.Element NameWhat it is: The full common name of the element (e.g., Carbon, Oxygen, Iron).Where it is: Usually displayed directly below the chemical symbol. (Note: Some compact or miniature periodic tables omit the full name to save space).Atomic Mass (or Atomic Weight)What it is: The weighted average mass of all the naturally occurring isotopes of that element, measured in atomic mass units (amu).Where it is: Located near the bottom of the tile, often presented as a decimal number.Additional Information (Found in Advanced Periodic Tables)Depending on the specific periodic table, tiles may also contain supplementary data:Electron Configuration: Shows how electrons are distributed across the element's atomic orbitals (e.g., $1s^2 2s^2$).Oxidation States / Common Charges: Lists the electrical charges the element typically adopts when forming chemical compounds.State of Matter / Color Coding: The text color, background color, or state indicator often shows whether the element is a solid, liquid, or gas at room temperature, as well as its classification (such as metal, nonmetal, or metalloid).
